# How to Play — Rotatris Prototype (PC)

## Starting the game

1. Double-click the `.exe` to launch it.
2. Click into the game window once it opens — Windows only sends
   keyboard input to whichever window currently has focus, so if you
   click elsewhere first, the controls below won't respond.

That's it — the board, camera, and score panel all appear automatically
as soon as the game starts.

## The goal

A fixed **core cell** sits on the floor at the bottom-center of the board.
Standard falling blocks (tetrominoes) drop from above, one at a time, and
you steer them to build a structure around the core.

A **highlighted 5×5 zone** sits directly above the core. That zone must
never be left with a gap that's sealed off from above — do that, and it's
game over on the spot. Everywhere else on the board, gaps are fine.

## Controls

| Action                     | Key(s)                  |
|----------------------------|--------------------------|
| Move piece left            | `A` or `←`               |
| Move piece right           | `D` or `→`               |
| Soft drop (fall faster)    | `S` or `↓` (hold)        |
| Hard drop (drop instantly) | `Space`                  |
| Rotate clockwise           | `W`, `X`, or `↑`         |
| Rotate counter-clockwise   | `Z`                      |
| Hold / swap piece          | `C`                      |
| Pause / resume             | `P` or `Esc`             |
| Restart (after game over)  | `R`                      |

## Reading the screen

- **Colored squares** on the board = locked, permanent structure. The
  off-white square at the bottom-center is the core — it never moves.
- **Solid colored piece** = the block you're currently controlling.
- **Faint, transparent copy of the piece below it** = the ghost preview,
  showing exactly where it will land if you don't move it further.
- **Faint yellow-tinted 5×5 area above the core** = the target zone — keep
  it gap-free.
- **Right-side panel** = Score, Best (your all-time high, saved between
  sessions), your currently held piece, and the next 3 pieces coming up.

## Tips

- Use **Hold** (`C`) to bank an awkward piece for later — you can only
  hold once per piece, until the next one locks in.
- Watch the **ghost preview**, not just the falling piece — it tells you
  exactly where a piece will settle before you commit.
- A piece that bridges over an open column can trap air underneath it —
  that's fine almost everywhere, but fatal if it happens inside the
  highlighted zone.
- If controls stop responding but the piece is still falling on its own,
  make sure the game window is focused/clicked — that's the most common
  cause. If it still doesn't respond, the project's Input settings may
  need adjusting before rebuilding (see the developer `README.md`'s
  Troubleshooting section).

## Ending the game

The game ends when either:
1. A new piece has nowhere valid to appear (the stack reached the top), or
2. A piece locks in a way that seals a gap inside the highlighted zone.

Press **R** at the game-over screen to start a new run instantly.
